[size=5][b]Loud and Clear[/b][/size]

Can't hear a word anyone's saying over the engines? This fixes it.

Galactic Racer has a great deal of dialogue, and a mix that buries most of it. Race chatter, commentary and story voices all compete with engines, crowds, airflow and music, and the in-game speech slider runs out of room long before the problem is solved. Loud and Clear lifts the voices and pulls the noise down around them.

[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Boosts dialogue, commentary and character voices to [b]1.7x[/b]
[*]Ducks music by 4.4 dB, crowds and airflow by 3.7 dB, rival engines by 3.1 dB, your own engine and exhaust by 2.5 dB, and ambience by 1.9 dB
[*]Applies itself a few seconds into every launch, with nothing to press
[*]Everything is tunable, and tunable live from the console with no restart
[*]An in-game panel on [b]HOME[/b] with live sliders for all of it, so you can tune by ear while you listen
[/list]

It only touches volume. No gameplay, saves, visuals or files inside the game are modified.

[size=4][b]Please read before installing[/b][/size]

[b]This has been tested in game, but not through full races.[/b] The game pushes its own mix changes during a race, ducking and lifting things as events happen, and how that interacts with these settings is unverified. It may be fine. It may need the numbers adjusted. Feedback from actual racing is welcome.

[b]Verified against Steam build 25801363 (8 Oct 2026).[/b] That patch moved two of the addresses UE4SS uses to find the engine. If UE4SS started crashing for you after it, that is why: the signature files some packages ship hardcode those addresses, so they resolve into the middle of other code while still reporting a successful scan. Replacements that find them by content are in the GitHub repo under Signatures/.

[b]I still can't guarantee fixes if a future update breaks this.[/b] UE4SS finds the engine by scanning for byte patterns, and a patch can move them. That is exactly why it's open source: the repo includes the tools to check whether an update broke anything before you launch, the reasoning behind every value, and notes on the game's audio layout so anyone can pick it up and fix it without starting from scratch.

[size=4][b]Known issue: the panel can hang the game[/b][/size]

[b]The game can freeze while the mix panel is in use, and twice it has frozen a few seconds after the panel was closed.[/b] Three occurrences so far, intermittent, and the cause is not yet established.

The audio side on its own has run for a day without a hang, so if you hit this, open [font=Courier New]Scripts\main.lua[/font] and set:

[code]panel_enabled = false,[/code]

That removes the panel and the timer behind it. Your mix is completely unaffected: everything still applies on launch, and the console commands still work for tuning.

A hang dump has been captured and analysed. It shows the game's main thread blocked with 32 threads parked in one identical wait, which is suggestive but not conclusive, since idle worker threads look the same. Investigation is ongoing and tracked on GitHub.

If you hit it and want to help, a dump is genuinely useful. Run [font=Courier New]procdump64.exe -h -n 3 -w SWGR-Win64-Shipping.exe C:\temp\dumps[/font] before launching and attach whatever it captures to the GitHub issue.

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]UE4SS with Unreal Engine 5.6 support.[/b] This matters. Older builds fail before any of this loads, with a signature scan that cannot find the engine internals. Packaging date is not a reliable signal: a package rebuilt recently for a different game can still contain a two year old DLL, so check the DLL itself.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install UE4SS into [font=Courier New]STAR WARS Galactic Racer\Griffin\Binaries\Win64[/font] (you should end up with [font=Courier New]dwmapi.dll[/font] and a [font=Courier New]ue4ss[/font] folder next to [font=Courier New]SWGR-Win64-Shipping.exe[/font])
[*]Copy the [font=Courier New]LoudAndClear[/font] folder into [font=Courier New]ue4ss\Mods[/font]
[*]Add the line [font=Courier New]LoudAndClear : 1[/font] to [font=Courier New]ue4ss\Mods\mods.txt[/font]
[*]Launch the game
[/list]

[size=4][b]Tuning[/b][/size]

Press [b]HOME[/b] in game. Nine sliders: voice level at the top, then the seven channels that compete with it, each showing its live value in dB. [b]Everything applies as you drag[/b], so you hear every change while you make it.

[list]
[*][b]Save[/b] keeps the current values for future launches
[*][b]Undo[/b] goes back to the last saved values
[*][b]Mod defaults[/b] goes back to what the mod ships with
[*][b]Bypass[/b] toggles the game's own unmodified mix
[*][b]Close[/b], or HOME again
[/list]

Mouse works, or arrow keys and Enter.

[b]Use Bypass to check a change is actually better.[/b] Toggling between your mix and the game's is the fastest way to tell, and far more reliable than trying to remember what the last value sounded like. Dragging a slider lifts Bypass automatically.

[b]How to tune it:[/b] open the panel somewhere with dialogue playing, raise Voice level until speech is clear, then pull down whatever is masking it (music and crowds in menus and cutscenes, airflow and engines during a race). Hit Bypass twice to confirm you improved things rather than just made them louder, then Save.

Two things worth knowing. If raising Voice level starts sounding harsh rather than louder, you are driving the compressor on the game's main bus; back it off and take the rest from the channels below. And engines below about half costs the racing noticeable weight, which is a real trade rather than a free win.

The panel takes input focus while open, so open it paused or in a menu rather than mid-race.

You can also use the UE4SS console, which reaches channels the panel does not list:

[code]lac_set Submixes/SS_Music 0.45
lac_set Submixes/SS_Crowds 0.50
lac_set Submixes/SS_Vehicles 0.55
lac_reset[/code]

Those take effect immediately. When you find values you like, put them in the [font=Courier New]duck[/font] table in the [font=Courier New]CONFIG[/font] block at the top of [font=Courier New]Scripts\main.lua[/font] so they persist.

For dialogue level itself, change [font=Courier New]class_boost[/font], which sits just above [font=Courier New]duck[/font] in the same [font=Courier New]CONFIG[/font] block. [font=Courier New]1.0[/font] is untouched, [font=Courier New]2.0[/font] is twice as loud.

[b]Keys:[/b] Ctrl+F7 re-apply, Ctrl+F8 dump the audio graph to the log, Ctrl+F9 restore the game's own levels, Ctrl+R reload after editing.

[b]Commands:[/b] [font=Courier New]lac_apply[/font], [font=Courier New]lac_reset[/font], [font=Courier New]lac_set[/font], [font=Courier New]lac_verify[/font], [font=Courier New]lac_dump[/font], [font=Courier New]lac_param[/font], [font=Courier New]lac_forget[/font]

[size=4][b]Why it ducks instead of only boosting[/b][/size]

For anyone wondering why the numbers look lopsided.

The game's volume controls run through Unreal's Audio Modulation system, and the parameter behind them treats 0 dB as both normal volume and its hard ceiling. Ask it for more and the request is silently discarded. So the obvious approach, turning dialogue up through the same path the in-game slider uses, cannot work no matter what value you put in.

Dialogue is instead lifted at the sound class stage, which is a separate control with no such ceiling, and the noise around it is pulled down through the modulation buses, which have 60 dB of range going downwards. The mod reads its own changes back after writing them and reports in the log whether each one actually took, rather than assuming.

There is also a compressor on the game's main output, so lifting voices a long way would pump the rest of the mix rather than making speech clearer. Pulling the maskers down avoids that, which is why ducking is doing most of the work.

[size=4][b]Compatibility and cautions[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Treat this as single player.[/b] The game has multiplayer through EOS and PlayFab. There is no anticheat, but an injected DLL is a terms of service question regardless, and the developer's crash reporting may see it.
[*][b]Re-check after a game update.[/b] UE4SS finds the engine by scanning for byte patterns, and a patch can move them. The repo includes [font=Courier New]check-signatures.py[/font] and [font=Courier New]check-after-update.ps1[/font] to verify before you launch. Also run [font=Courier New]lac_forget[/font] after a patch, since an update can rebalance the mix.
[*][b]Should not conflict[/b] with other audio mods unless they touch the same submixes or sound classes.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Troubleshooting[/b][/size]

Everything the mod does is logged to [font=Courier New]ue4ss\UE4SS.log[/font]. A healthy launch looks like this:

[code]PS scan successful
Starting Lua mod 'LoudAndClear'
restored 2 authored class volumes
audio graph is up after 6s, applying
class SC_Voice  1.000 -> 1.700  (x1.70, verified)
applied: 7/7 buses ducked, 2/2 class volumes boosted[/code]

[b]No [font=Courier New]LoudAndClear[/font] lines at all[/b], and a [font=Courier New]PS scan timed out[/font] or [font=Courier New]Failed to find GMalloc[/font] above them, means the UE4SS build is too old for this game's engine version.

[b]A fatal error naming another game's executable[/b] means that package left game-specific signature files in [font=Courier New]ue4ss\UE4SS_Signatures[/font]. Move them out of that folder.

[b]Dialogue level unchanged[/b] but the log says [font=Courier New]verified[/font]: sound class volume is sampled when a line starts, so a line already playing won't change. Trigger a new one.

[size=4][b]Source[/b][/size]

[url=https://github.com/DatGuySnowfox/swgr-loud-and-clear]github.com/DatGuySnowfox/swgr-loud-and-clear[/url]

Full notes on the game's audio architecture are in the README, including the submix and sound class routing, which stages respond and which don't, and the reasoning above in more detail. Useful if you want to mod this game's audio yourself.
